{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for lane L at exact head 225997839a62fbe34d04c0e7ee6e33ed5f1c10f2, resting on all 27 artifacts.\n\nP1 — Own the publication cohort before asynchronous I/O (packages/log/src/publisher.ts:118–170). The Git tree is built before push, but done() later copies the caller's live entries array. I paused push for a cohort ending at seq 2, appended a valid seq 3, then released it. The commit contains only 0–2, while publisher.publishedThrough becomes 3. A second call returns that same commit with through:3 and attempts:0. This can make the Room checkpoint an entry that was never published. Copy the canonical entries, checkpoint and retained data before the first await, and update state solely from those owned bytes. Test appending and mutating caller objects during both push and readback; the next cohort must actually publish its new entries.\n\nP1 — Check delegation kinds when the grant is admitted (packages/log/src/roster.ts:106–172,212). A member can grant check permission, later be promoted to checker, and have the grantee use the invalid grant successfully. A fully signed transcript containing that sequence verifies ok:true with no failures. The later current-role check cannot validate a forbidden original grant. Enforce R-ADM-5 at the delegate entry, expanding wildcard kinds consistently with the contract, and stop the verified prefix there with a named authority reason. Preserve current-role, expiry and revocation checks when the grant is used. Amendment 2 also identifies this as mandatory, rather than an optional gap.\n\nP2 — Report malformed log content instead of throwing (packages/log/src/verify.ts:221–226 and the untrusted decoding boundary). A readable Git commit whose first JSONL entry is {\"seq\":0} makes verifyLog reject with TypeError at g0.entry.type. The CLI then classifies this as a fetch/usage failure rather than reporting the corrupt prefix. Validate untrusted entry/envelope/checkpoint/context shapes before dereferencing them and return a named verification failure with the last valid prefix. Add malformed first and later entries, malformed signatures and retained data cases; preserve genuine I/O errors as distinct failures.\n\nP2 — Preserve previously published replay evidence (packages/log/src/publisher.ts:135 and entries.ts logFiles). I published a valid prefix with retained policies and inputs, appended a valid lease-expired event, then called publish(entries, checkpoint) with its default empty retained argument. The forward commit kept the old entry lines but removed their retained evidence; a previously valid log now fails policy-missing at seq 1. Merge immutable historical retained objects from the last published tree, including after open(), or reject an incomplete cohort before pushing with an explicit API contract. Test omitted historical policies/inputs across later publication and publisher restart. Published prefixes must remain independently replayable.\n\nP2 — Replay delayed notifications with their pinned policy (packages/log/src/verify.ts:282–286). The verifier requires every decision's policy to equal the active policy at the later notified entry. A claim evaluated under policy 1, activation of policy 3, then a notified entry using the original queued context fails policy-version-mismatch. This matches the actual Room queue: lane A core.ts enqueueNotify stores policy.version and drainNotify retrieves policyAt(row.policy). Bind notification replay to the referenced act's queued evaluation policy and retained context, while rejecting unrelated or nonexistent versions. Test policy activation between queueing and sealing, retry under the same original version, and substitution with a wrong older policy.\n\nValidation: candidate root typecheck passed; candidate tests passed 33 on Node and 29 in workerd, including the local Git/CLI round trips. Five independent diagnostics reproduced the findings on both Node and workerd. All production/test/config files and both live-roundtrip drivers were reviewed. The README's live Artifacts timings are builder-reported; I did not repeat deployment, token creation or repository cleanup.\n\nArchitecture: the acyclic checkpoint scheme, content-addressed replay data and fast-forward lease are useful foundations. Publication state must reflect an immutable committed cohort, and every later published prefix must keep its replay evidence. Cross-lane queued-policy semantics must agree.\n\nSecurity: signatures, roster replay and revocation checks give meaningful verification, but admitting forbidden delegation grants violates the authority contract. Malformed attacker-controlled files need structured rejection.\n\nSimplification: one owned publication snapshot and one validated decoding boundary are clearer than repairs scattered across push outcomes and verification branches. Keep deterministic replay version selection explicit per event kind. Git-commit signatures, checker digests and state re-derivation are separately addressed by pending contract amendment 2; they are not silently considered completed in this verdict.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a54cf525d246ae939108f3dddfcd9e64dade7412","binding":"assigned","head":"225997839a62fbe34d04c0e7ee6e33ed5f1c10f2","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d83fdd1620c777c4389b0745d590b4eb591f61fd\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:26f088c3bc7090e81699ffd310739a4424989b87\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7ae2bcd08aabed5f1f0d7c85612e84ef5ba40e16","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}